.\" more.1 -- maux's more(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An original
.\" page (V7 had no more; this describes maux's own POSIX-shaped
.\" one), so it carries no third-party notice -- MIT like the code.
.\" Rendered at build time to build/man/MORE.TXT and staged on the
.\" play image as /usr/man/MORE: `more /usr/man/MORE`.
.TH MORE 1 "September 12, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
more \- page through a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B more
[
.B \-ceisu
] [
.B \-n
lines ] [ file ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I More
displays the named
.I files
(standard input default) on the console a screenful at a time,
pausing after each with a
.B \-\-More\-\-
prompt.
A single key at the prompt says what to do next; the keys are
those of POSIX
.IR more ,
and most take a
.I count
typed first.
Every line's position is remembered, so the display can also go
backward.
When standard output is not the console the files are simply
copied to it and only
.B \-s
has any effect.
.PP
The options are
.TP
.B \-c
Clear the screen before each file rather than scrolling.
A full page forward is always painted from the top of a cleared
screen; a half page or a counted number of lines scrolls.
.TP
.B \-e
Exit when the last line of the last file has been written,
instead of prompting there.
.TP
.B \-i
Ignore case in searches.
.TP
.BI \-n " lines"
Use
.I lines
as the screenful, instead of one less than the console's rows.
.TP
.B \-s
Squeeze runs of empty lines to one.
.TP
.B \-u
Leave backspaces alone.
Without it a character overstruck with itself (as
.IR nroff (1)
emboldens) is shown bold, and one overstruck with an underscore
plain.
.TP
.B \-A
Page on the terminal's alternate screen: the screen as it was
before
.I more
started comes back when it exits, as
.IR vi (1)
does. Not in POSIX;
.IR man (1)
uses it, so a manual page leaves no trace.
.PP
The commands are
.TP
.BR SPACE ", " f ", ^F"
Forward a screenful; with a count, that many lines
.RB ( f :
past the screen, only the last screenful).
.TP
.BR RETURN ", " j
Forward one line, or
.I count
lines.
.TP
.B k
Back one line, or
.I count
lines.
.TP
.BR d ", ^D"
Forward half a screenful.
A count becomes the new half.
.TP
.BR u ", ^U"
Back half a screenful.
.TP
.BR b ", ^B"
Back a screenful, or
.I count
lines.
.TP
.B s
Skip
.I count
lines (one by default) and show a screenful.
.TP
.B g
Go to line
.I count
(the first by default).
.TP
.B G
Go to line
.IR count ,
or to the last screenful.
.TP
.BI / pattern
Search forward for the
.IR count th
line matching the regular expression (the basic regular
expressions of
.IR ed (1)),
and show a screenful starting there.
A leading `!' finds a line
.I not
matching; an empty pattern repeats the last one.
.TP
.BI ? pattern
The same, searching backward.
.TP
.BR n ", " N
Repeat the last search in the same, or the opposite, direction.
.TP
.BI m letter
Mark the current position with a lower-case
.IR letter .
.TP
.BI \(aa letter
Return to that mark; `\(aa\(aa' returns to where the last large
movement started.
.TP
.BR = ", ^G"
Show the file name, line number, and how far through the file
the display is.
.TP
.BR r ", ^L"
Redraw the screen.
.TP
.BR :n ", " :p
The next, or previous, file named on the command line.
.TP
.BI :e " file"
Examine
.IR file .
.TP
.B v
Edit the current file with
.IR vi (1),
then continue.
.TP
.BI ! command
Run
.I command
through
.IR sh (1).
.TP
.B h
A summary of the commands.
.TP
.BR q ", " :q ", " ZZ
Quit.
.PP
At the end of a file the prompt says so, or names the next file;
a forward command then goes on to it, or, after the last file,
quits.
A file that fits on one screen is shown and
.I more
exits.
The arrow keys act as
.BR k ,
.BR j ,
SPACE and
.BR b .
.SH FILES
.TP
MORE.INxx
where a pipe on standard input is kept while it is paged (xx
names the process), removed on exit
.TP
MORE.CMDxx
the `!' command on its way to the shell
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cat(1), sed(1), vi(1), ed(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0, or 1 when a file could not be opened, or 2 on
a usage error.
.SH BUGS
A line longer than 255 characters is searched only in its first
255.
Bold on the Apple //e's own text page shows as inverse, and
inverse lower case is not in that character set; on the
UltraTerm it is bright.
The line table holds 10000 lines; past that the rest of a file
is out of reach.
